-- Q1: medium / L2 — Equity method income exceeds dividends received
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Hargrove Industries holds a 30% equity-method investment in Dalton Corp. During Year 2, Hargrove recognizes $240,000 of equity-method income and receives $90,000 in cash dividends from Dalton. Under the indirect method, what adjustment is required in the operating activities section for this investment?', '["Add $240,000 and subtract $90,000", "Subtract $150,000 from net income", "Add $90,000 to net income", "Subtract $240,000 and add $90,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, equity-method income that exceeds dividends received is a noncash component of net income. Net income includes the full $240,000 equity income, but only $90,000 was received in cash. The $150,000 excess ($240,000 − $90,000) must be subtracted from net income under the indirect method to remove the noncash portion. Choice A incorrectly applies gross adjustments rather than the net excess approach typically presented; the net $150,000 subtraction properly reconciles accrual income to cash.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Operating lease ROU amortization vs finance lease depreciation in CF
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Shelton Logistics has two leases: an operating lease with $48,000 annual ROU amortization and a finance lease with $35,000 annual ROU depreciation. Both amounts are included in operating expenses on the income statement. Under the indirect method, analyze how each lease''s noncash amortization is treated in the operating activities section.', '["Both the $48,000 and $35,000 are added back to net income as noncash charges", "Only the $35,000 finance lease depreciation is added back; the operating lease amortization is not adjusted", "Only the $48,000 operating lease amortization is added back; the finance lease depreciation requires no adjustment", "Neither amount is added back because both are included in lease liability reductions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 842 and ASC 230-10-45-28, both the operating lease ROU amortization ($48,000) and the finance lease ROU depreciation ($35,000) are noncash charges that reduced net income without consuming cash. Both must be added back to net income under the indirect method. For the operating lease, the ROU asset amortization is a distinct noncash item separate from the lease liability reduction (which reflects the principal portion of the cash payment). For the finance lease, ROU depreciation is treated identically to PP&E depreciation. Choice B is incorrect because it confuses the operating lease liability reduction (a cash-related working capital change) with the ROU amortization (a noncash charge); these are independent adjustments in the indirect method reconciliation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Goodwill impairment noncash add-back
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Westbrook Holdings recognizes a $500,000 goodwill impairment loss in Year 2, reducing net income to $180,000. The controller prepares the statement of cash flows using the indirect method. What adjustment is made in the operating activities section for the impairment?', '["Subtract $500,000 from net income", "No adjustment is necessary because goodwill is an investing item", "Report the $500,000 as an investing outflow", "Add $500,000 to net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, goodwill impairment is a noncash charge that reduced net income but did not use cash. It must be added back to net income in the operating activities section under the indirect method, similar to depreciation and amortization. Choice B is incorrect because while goodwill relates to a prior acquisition (investing), the impairment charge itself is a noncash income statement item that must be reversed in operating activities to reconcile to cash flows.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Contingent consideration payment post-acquisition classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Riverton Group acquired Beacon Corp. in Year 1 for $3,000,000 plus contingent consideration of up to $600,000 based on Year 2 earnings targets. At acquisition, the contingent liability was measured at $400,000 fair value. In Year 2, Beacon meets the target and Riverton pays $550,000. Under ASC 230, evaluate the cash flow classification of the $550,000 payment, considering the original $400,000 acquisition-date fair value.', '["The entire $550,000 is a financing outflow", "The entire $550,000 is an investing outflow since it relates to the acquisition", "$400,000 is a financing outflow and $150,000 is an operating outflow", "$400,000 is an investing outflow and $150,000 is an operating outflow"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-21A, contingent consideration payments made after a business combination are bifurcated: payments up to the acquisition-date fair value ($400,000) are classified as financing outflows, and amounts exceeding that ($150,000) are classified as operating outflows. Choice B incorrectly treats the full payment as investing; ASC 230 specifically requires the financing/operating split. Choice D incorrectly uses investing for the base amount—the standard designates financing, not investing, for the acquisition-date portion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Interest received classification under GAAP
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Under U.S. GAAP, how is interest received on a bond investment classified on the statement of cash flows?', '["Investing activity", "Financing activity", "Operating activity", "Noncash supplemental disclosure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-16, interest received is classified as an operating activity under U.S. GAAP for nonfinancial companies, consistent with the classification of interest paid. This differs from IFRS, which allows interest received to be classified as either operating or investing. Choice A is incorrect because only the purchase or sale of the investment itself is an investing activity, not the periodic interest income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Hedging derivative cash flow classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Kingsley Manufacturing enters a cash flow hedge using an interest rate swap to hedge variable-rate debt payments. During Year 2, Kingsley makes $85,000 in net swap settlement payments. The CFO classifies all swap payments as operating activities because they relate to interest expense. The treasurer argues the payments should follow the hedged item''s classification. Evaluate the proper classification under ASC 230.', '["Operating activities, because all derivative settlements are operating by default", "Financing activities, because the swap hedges financing-related debt payments", "Operating activities, consistent with the interest expense on the hedged variable-rate debt", "Investing activities, because derivative instruments are financial assets"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-27A, cash flows from derivative instruments that are designated as hedges are classified in the same category as the cash flows from the hedged item. Since interest payments on debt are operating activities under U.S. GAAP, the swap settlement payments are also classified as operating. The CFO''s conclusion is correct but the reasoning is incomplete—it is not a default rule but the hedged-item-matching principle. Choice B is incorrect because while the debt itself is financing, the interest payments (the hedged cash flows) are operating.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Pension expense vs cash contribution adjustment
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Aldrich Corp. reports pension expense of $320,000 in Year 2, which includes service cost, interest cost, and expected return on plan assets. The company contributed $250,000 in cash to the pension fund during the year. Under the indirect method, analyze the appropriate adjustment in the operating activities section.', '["Add $320,000 and subtract $250,000 from net income", "Add $70,000 to net income because pension expense exceeded contributions", "Subtract $70,000 from net income because contributions were less than expense", "No adjustment because pension expense equals the cash cost"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230, pension expense is a noncash charge that reduces net income, while the cash contribution is the actual cash outflow. The indirect method adds back the full $320,000 noncash expense and then subtracts the $250,000 cash contribution, producing a net $70,000 add-back. This is functionally equivalent to Choice B, but the standard approach reflects the gross adjustment through the accrued pension liability change. Choice C reverses the direction; when expense exceeds contributions, the accrued liability increases, requiring an addition to net income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: hard / L3 — Life insurance proceeds classification (COLI death benefit)
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Pemberton Industries holds company-owned life insurance (COLI) policies on key executives. During Year 2, one executive passes away and Pemberton receives a $750,000 death benefit. The policy had a cash surrender value of $290,000 at the date of death. The $460,000 excess over CSV is reported as other income. Analyze the proper cash flow classification of the $750,000 proceeds under ASC 230.', '["$750,000 as an operating inflow because it relates to an insurance contract", "$290,000 as an investing inflow and $460,000 as an operating inflow", "$750,000 as an investing inflow because COLI is an investment asset", "$460,000 as an investing inflow and $290,000 as an operating inflow"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-12(d), proceeds from insurance settlements are classified based on the nature of the related item. COLI policies are investment assets, so the entire $750,000 death benefit is classified as an investing inflow. Under the indirect method, the $460,000 gain included in net income would be subtracted from operating activities to avoid double-counting. Choice B incorrectly splits the proceeds; ASC 230 classifies the full insurance proceeds as investing regardless of the CSV/gain breakdown.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Amortization of intangible assets in indirect method
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Under the indirect method, how is amortization of a patent treated in the operating activities section?', '["Subtracted from net income", "Added to net income", "Reported as an investing outflow", "Excluded from the statement entirely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, amortization of intangible assets such as patents is a noncash expense that reduced net income without using cash. It is added back to net income in the operating activities section, identical to the treatment of depreciation. Choice A is incorrect because subtracting a noncash expense would understate operating cash flows; the purpose of the add-back is to reverse charges that did not consume cash.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Inventory increase effect on operating cash flows
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Redmond Supply reports net income of $275,000 for Year 2. Inventory increased from $180,000 to $245,000 during the year. All other working capital accounts remained unchanged, and depreciation was $30,000. What is cash from operating activities under the indirect method?', '["$370,000", "$240,000", "$340,000", "$210,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, an increase in inventory ($245,000 − $180,000 = $65,000) represents cash spent to acquire goods not yet sold, requiring a subtraction from net income under the indirect method. Cash from operations = $275,000 + $30,000 depreciation − $65,000 inventory increase = $240,000. Choice C incorrectly adds the inventory change rather than subtracting it. An inventory increase means the company paid more cash for inventory than was recognized in cost of goods sold.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Proceeds from stock option exercises classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Under ASC 230, cash received from employees exercising stock options is classified as which type of activity?', '["Operating activity", "Investing activity", "Financing activity", "Noncash supplemental disclosure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-14(a), proceeds from issuing equity instruments, including stock option exercises, are classified as financing activities. The company receives cash in exchange for issuing shares, which is a transaction with owners. Choice A is incorrect because operating activities relate to the entity''s core business operations, not capital transactions with equity holders.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Factoring without recourse vs with recourse CF impact
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Dunmore Textiles has $600,000 in accounts receivable. In Scenario A, Dunmore factors the receivables without recourse for $570,000, qualifying as a sale under ASC 860. In Scenario B, Dunmore factors with full recourse and retains substantially all risks, qualifying as a secured borrowing. The CFO wants to understand how each scenario affects the statement of cash flows. Evaluate the classification differences between the two scenarios.', '["Both are operating inflows because they involve trade receivables", "Scenario A is an operating inflow of $570,000; Scenario B is a financing inflow of $570,000", "Scenario A is an investing inflow; Scenario B is an operating inflow", "Both are financing inflows because factoring is a form of borrowing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 860 and ASC 230, the classification depends on whether the transfer qualifies as a sale. In Scenario A (without recourse, qualifying as a sale), the $570,000 is an operating cash inflow because the receivables are derecognized and the cash replaces the normal collection cycle. In Scenario B (with recourse, secured borrowing), the $570,000 is a financing inflow because the receivables remain on the balance sheet and the advance is effectively a loan. Choice D incorrectly treats both as financing; only the secured borrowing scenario is financing.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Cash paid for lawsuit settlement classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Under ASC 230, how is a cash payment to settle a product liability lawsuit typically classified on the statement of cash flows?', '["Financing activity", "Investing activity", "Noncash disclosure item", "Operating activity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-17(d), cash payments to settle litigation claims arising from the entity''s operations are classified as operating activities. Lawsuit settlements related to the company''s products are an operational cost. Choice A is incorrect because financing activities involve transactions with creditors and owners, not operational legal claims. The default classification rule under ASC 230 places items in operating activities unless they clearly relate to investing or financing.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Warranty accrual vs warranty payments in indirect method
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Tremaine Electronics accrues $180,000 in assurance-type warranty expense during Year 2, increasing the warranty liability. Actual warranty claims paid in cash during the year total $145,000. Under the indirect method, analyze the net effect on the operating activities section from the warranty activity.', '["Add $35,000 to net income", "Subtract $35,000 from net income", "Add $180,000 and subtract $145,000 from net income separately", "No adjustment is needed because warranty costs are already in net income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 230, the indirect method adjusts for changes in operating liabilities. The warranty liability increased by $35,000 ($180,000 accrued − $145,000 paid), and increases in operating liabilities are added to net income. This reflects that cash outflows ($145,000) were less than the warranty expense ($180,000) recognized in net income. Choice D is incorrect because while warranty expense is in net income, the difference between accrual and cash payment must be reconciled through the liability change.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Increase in prepaid insurance effect on cash flows
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Carrington Services reports net income of $195,000 for Year 2. During the year, prepaid insurance increased from $22,000 to $38,000 while all other balance sheet accounts remained constant except for the $16,000 cash decrease. Under the indirect method, analyze why the prepaid increase reduces cash from operating activities.', '["The $16,000 increase is subtracted because it represents cash paid in advance that was not expensed, meaning cash outflows exceeded the insurance expense in net income", "The $16,000 increase is added because prepaid insurance is an asset that will generate future benefit", "The $16,000 increase is reported as an investing outflow because insurance is a long-term asset", "No adjustment is needed because insurance expense was already deducted in computing net income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, an increase in prepaid expenses means the company paid more cash for insurance than was recognized as expense in the income statement. The $16,000 increase is subtracted from net income because cash outflows ($16,000 more) exceeded the expense recognized. Cash from operations = $195,000 − $16,000 = $179,000. Choice B incorrectly adds the increase; asset increases represent cash used, not cash generated.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L2 — Proceeds from sale of AFS securities classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Calloway Financial sells available-for-sale debt securities with a carrying value of $200,000 for $215,000 cash. The $15,000 gain was previously in accumulated other comprehensive income. Under ASC 230, what is the cash flow classification of the $215,000 proceeds?', '["$200,000 investing inflow and $15,000 operating inflow", "Operating inflow of $215,000", "$215,000 investing inflow", "$15,000 financing inflow and $200,000 investing inflow"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-12, proceeds from the sale of investments are classified as investing activities. The full $215,000 cash received is reported as an investing inflow. Under the indirect method, the $15,000 gain reclassified from OCI to net income would be subtracted from operating activities to avoid double-counting. Choice A incorrectly splits the proceeds; the entire cash amount flows through investing regardless of the gain component.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Tax refund received classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Garrison Corp. receives a $125,000 income tax refund in Year 2 resulting from an amended prior-year return. The refund is not specifically identifiable with any investing or financing transaction. Under ASC 230, how should Garrison classify this cash receipt?', '["Financing inflow", "Operating inflow", "Investing inflow", "Reported as a separate reconciling item outside the three categories"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-17(e), income tax payments and refunds are classified as operating activities unless they can be specifically identified with a financing or investing activity. Since this refund relates to a general prior-year return with no specific investing or financing nexus, it is an operating inflow. Choice A is incorrect because financing activities involve transactions with creditors and owners, not tax authority refunds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Depreciation in direct method presentation
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'When preparing the operating activities section using the direct method, how is depreciation expense presented?', '["Added to net income as the starting point", "Listed as a separate operating cash outflow", "Not reported in the operating section because it is a noncash item", "Subtracted from total cash receipts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-25, the direct method reports major classes of gross operating cash receipts and payments. Depreciation is not reported in the operating section under the direct method because it does not involve a cash payment. Choice A describes the indirect method treatment, not the direct method. Choice B is incorrect because depreciation never represents a cash outflow under either method.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Deferred revenue decrease effect on operating CF
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Stratton Media reports net income of $310,000 for Year 2. During the year, deferred subscription revenue decreased from $120,000 to $85,000 as the company recognized previously collected cash as revenue. Under the indirect method, analyze the effect of this deferred revenue change on cash from operating activities.', '["Add $35,000 because the liability decreased, indicating strong cash collection", "Subtract $35,000 because the decrease means revenue was recognized without corresponding cash collection in the current period", "No adjustment is needed because the revenue is already included in net income", "Add $35,000 because deferred revenue is a noncash item like depreciation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, a decrease in deferred revenue means the company recognized $35,000 more revenue than it collected in cash during the period (the cash was collected in a prior period). This overstates the cash-generating power of current net income, so $35,000 is subtracted. Decreases in operating liabilities are subtracted under the indirect method. Choice A incorrectly adds the change; a liability decrease signals cash outflow exceeded the related expense, or revenue exceeded current cash receipts.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Debt issuance costs presentation under ASU 2015-03
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Ridgeline Corp. issues $5,000,000 in 10-year bonds at par on March 1, 2026, and pays $120,000 in debt issuance costs to underwriters at closing. Under ASU 2015-03, the issuance costs are netted against the bond liability on the balance sheet. The CFO proposes reporting a net $4,880,000 financing inflow. The controller argues that ASC 230 requires gross presentation of the $5,000,000 inflow and a separate $120,000 financing outflow. Evaluate which approach is correct.', '["Net $4,880,000 financing inflow is correct because ASU 2015-03 requires netting on both the balance sheet and cash flow statement", "Gross presentation: $5,000,000 financing inflow and $120,000 financing outflow", "Either approach is acceptable under ASC 230", "Net $4,880,000 financing inflow and $120,000 operating outflow"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 230 does not prescribe whether debt issuance costs must be presented gross or net in financing activities. Both approaches are acceptable in practice. ASU 2015-03 (ASC 835-30-45) requires netting on the balance sheet, but this balance sheet presentation requirement does not mandate the same treatment on the cash flow statement. Choice A incorrectly assumes the balance sheet presentation dictates cash flow presentation. Choice D incorrectly classifies issuance costs as operating; they are directly related to a financing transaction.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Foreign subsidiary cash flow translation and reconciliation
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Blackwell Corp. consolidates a Japanese subsidiary whose functional currency is the yen. The subsidiary reports operating cash flows of ¥50,000,000. The average exchange rate during Year 2 is $0.0072/¥, the beginning rate is $0.0070/¥, and the ending rate is $0.0075/¥. Blackwell''s consolidated statement shows an "Effect of exchange rate changes on cash" line. Evaluate how the subsidiary''s operating cash flows are translated and what the exchange rate effect line captures.', '["Operating cash flows are translated at the ending rate of $0.0075, and the exchange rate line captures the difference from the average rate", "Operating cash flows are translated at the average rate of $0.0072, and the exchange rate line reconciles the difference between translating beginning and ending cash balances at their respective spot rates versus the average rate used for flows", "Operating cash flows are translated at the beginning rate, and the exchange rate line captures the total currency movement", "All subsidiary cash flows are translated at the ending rate, so no exchange rate effect line is needed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 830-230-45-1, a foreign subsidiary''s cash flows are translated at the exchange rates in effect at the time of the cash flows (approximated by weighted-average rates). The "effect of exchange rate changes on cash" line reconciles the mathematical difference that arises because beginning cash was translated at the beginning spot rate, ending cash at the ending spot rate, but intervening flows at average rates. Choice A incorrectly uses the ending rate for operating flows; the average rate approximation is required for period flows.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Financing activity classification for debt repayment
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Under ASC 230, repayment of the principal portion of a bank loan is classified as which type of activity?', '["Operating activity", "Financing activity", "Investing activity", "It depends on the loan''s original purpose"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-15(b), repayments of amounts borrowed are classified as financing activities. Principal repayment on a bank loan reduces the entity''s outstanding debt, which is a transaction with creditors. Choice D is incorrect because the classification does not depend on how the loan proceeds were used; all principal debt repayments are financing regardless of the loan''s purpose.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Proceeds from sale-leaseback total CF effect
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Langford Corp. sells a manufacturing facility with a book value of $1,200,000 for $1,500,000 and simultaneously leases it back under an operating lease qualifying as a sale under ASC 842. Under ASC 230, what amount does Langford report as an investing inflow?', '["$1,200,000", "$300,000", "$0 because it is a noncash transaction", "$1,500,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-12 and ASC 842, when a sale-leaseback qualifies as a sale, the full $1,500,000 cash received is reported as an investing inflow. The $300,000 gain is included in net income and subtracted in the operating section under the indirect method to avoid double-counting. Choice A incorrectly limits the investing inflow to book value; the full cash proceeds, not the book value, are reported as the investing inflow.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Stock-based compensation and excess tax benefit CF
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Winfield Technologies reports $85,000 in stock-based compensation expense for Year 2 related to employee stock options. None of the options were exercised during the year. Under the indirect method, analyze the treatment of the stock-based compensation expense in the operating activities section and why it differs from depreciation despite both being noncash charges.', '["Subtract $85,000 because stock compensation is an equity transaction, not an operating charge", "Add $85,000 to net income—both are noncash charges added back, and the similarity to depreciation is that neither uses cash yet both reduce net income", "No adjustment is needed because stock compensation is already a noncash item excluded from net income", "Report $85,000 as a financing outflow because it relates to equity instruments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-28, stock-based compensation expense is a noncash charge added back to net income under the indirect method, identical in treatment to depreciation. Both reduce net income without consuming cash. The credit for stock compensation goes to additional paid-in capital (equity) rather than reducing an asset, but the cash flow treatment is the same: add back the noncash expense. Choice A incorrectly subtracts it; noncash expenses that reduced net income are always added back.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Escrow deposit classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Whitman Development places $350,000 into an escrow account as part of a real estate acquisition that is expected to close in 90 days. The funds are held by a third-party escrow agent and are restricted for the purchase. Under ASC 230, how should Whitman classify this cash outflow?', '["Operating outflow because escrow relates to business operations", "Financing outflow because it relates to a future commitment", "Investing outflow because it relates to the acquisition of a long-term asset", "No classification needed until the acquisition closes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-12, cash placed in escrow for the purchase of a long-term asset is classified as an investing outflow at the time the cash is deposited, not when the acquisition closes. The escrow represents a commitment of cash toward an asset purchase. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 230 requires classification when cash changes hands, not when the underlying transaction is consummated. The cash left the entity''s control when deposited with the escrow agent.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Supplemental disclosures required with indirect method
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'When a company uses the indirect method for operating activities, which supplemental disclosure is required under ASC 230?', '["A reconciliation of beginning and ending retained earnings", "The amounts of interest paid and income taxes paid during the period", "A schedule of all noncurrent asset purchases", "The fair value of all financial instruments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-50-2, when the indirect method is used, entities must separately disclose cash paid for interest and cash paid for income taxes. These amounts are embedded in operating activities but not separately visible under the indirect method. Choice A describes the statement of stockholders'' equity, not a cash flow disclosure. The direct method inherently shows interest and tax payments as line items, but the indirect method requires them as supplemental disclosures.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Capitalized interest impact on CF classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Merritt Construction capitalizes $95,000 of interest on a self-constructed warehouse during Year 2. Total interest paid during the year is $280,000 (including the $95,000 capitalized). The CFO reports $185,000 as an operating outflow and $95,000 as an investing outflow. Analyze whether this classification is appropriate under ASC 230.', '["Correct—capitalized interest should be split between operating and investing based on the nature of the expenditure", "Incorrect—ASC 230 requires that all interest paid, including capitalized amounts, be classified as operating", "Incorrect—all $280,000 should be classified as investing because it relates to asset construction", "Incorrect—capitalized interest is a noncash item and should be excluded from the cash flow statement entirely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-17(b), all interest paid is classified as an operating activity under U.S. GAAP, even amounts that are capitalized as part of a constructed asset''s cost. The full $280,000 must be reported as an operating outflow. The CFO''s proposed split is incorrect. Choice A incorrectly supports the split; ASC 230 does not permit bifurcation of interest payments between operating and investing regardless of capitalization.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Cash surrender value increase in COLI
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Evergreen Corp. holds company-owned life insurance (COLI) policies. During Year 2, the cash surrender value (CSV) increases by $40,000 from $210,000 to $250,000. Evergreen paid $55,000 in premiums during the year. Under the indirect method, what adjustment is required in operating activities for the COLI activity?', '["Add $40,000 to net income", "Subtract $40,000 from net income", "Add $55,000 to net income", "No adjustment because COLI is an investing asset"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230, the $40,000 CSV increase recognized in net income is a noncash gain—no cash was received from the increase in surrender value. It must be subtracted from net income under the indirect method. The $55,000 premium payment is the actual cash outflow, typically classified as investing (asset purchase). Choice A incorrectly adds the gain; noncash income items that increased net income must be subtracted to reconcile to actual cash flows. The difference between the premium paid and CSV increase is expensed.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Money market funds as cash equivalents
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Under ASC 230, which of the following is most likely classified as a cash equivalent?', '["A 120-day certificate of deposit", "A money market fund with daily liquidity", "Common stock of a publicly traded company", "A 6-month U.S. Treasury note"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-20, cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments with original maturities of three months or less that are readily convertible to known amounts of cash. Money market funds with daily liquidity meet all criteria. Choice A is incorrect because a 120-day CD exceeds the three-month threshold. Choice D is incorrect because a 6-month Treasury note exceeds the original maturity requirement, even though it is highly liquid.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Payment to retire preferred stock classification
(2, 'Statement of Cash Flows', 'Oakdale Industries redeems 5,000 shares of its callable preferred stock for $525,000, which includes the $500,000 par value plus a $25,000 call premium. Under ASC 230, how should Oakdale classify this payment?', '["$500,000 as a financing outflow and $25,000 as an operating outflow", "Operating outflow of $525,000", "Financing outflow of $525,000", "$500,000 as a financing outflow and $25,000 as a noncash item"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-15(c), payments to retire equity instruments, including any call premium, are classified as financing activities. The entire $525,000 cash payment is a financing outflow. Choice A incorrectly splits the premium into operating; the call premium is part of the cost of retiring equity and is inseparable from the financing transaction. Redeeming preferred stock is a transaction with owners, placing it entirely in financing.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
